window.docContents[31].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Coverage-and-the-transition-to-fluids":{"contents":"Characteristics construct classical solutions, energy estimates control them,\nand entropy and viscosity conditions give meaning to equations after classical\ndifferentiability is lost. The theorem chains cover classical transport and waves,\nweak-wave evolution, selected semilinear energies, scalar Riemann problems,\nlocal system constructions, and one-dimensional Hopf–Lax evolution.\n\nFor Hamilton–Jacobi equations, the one-dimensional Hopf–Lax and\nautonomous-control constructions sit beside a finite-dimensional comparison\ntheorem. For continuous autonomous , viscosity sub- and supersolutions\ntested with local  functions preserve their initial ordering when both\nare bounded and uniformly continuous on every finite closed time slab. The\nsame holds for position-dependent  that is uniformly Lipschitz in\n with spatial variation bounded by . A scalar\ncoordinate change brings the one-dimensional differentiable-contact\npredicates into this comparison class. Perron's method gives continuous\nsolutions of the autonomous equation on compact spacetime sets between\nboundary barriers that agree on the frontier. For state-dependent optimal\ncontrol on a finite-dimensional state space, with bounded Lipschitz dynamics\nand a bounded Borel running cost that is Lipschitz in the state, the\nvalue function is the unique\nbounded uniformly continuous viscosity solution of the terminal\nHamilton–Jacobi–Bellman problem.\n\nFor conservation laws we have scalar Burgers solutions, local characteristic\nand wave-map geometry for systems, and a concrete p-system development.\nScalar entropy solutions with strong initial traces satisfy Kružkov's local\n contraction and are unique, the Lax–Oleinik formula gives weak\nsolutions for uniformly convex fluxes, and almost-everywhere limits of bounded\nviscous solutions are entropy solutions. For a general  strictly\nhyperbolic system whose families are each genuinely nonlinear or linearly\ndegenerate, the Riemann problem with nearby states has a local self-similar\nweak solution with a strong initial trace, glued from elementary connections.\n\nOn nonlinear waves, the energy identities and finite-propagation estimates\napply to given regular solutions. There is also a construction on a\nnonempty bounded open domain: symmetric, bounded, uniformly elliptic\ncoefficients give a linear Dirichlet wave evolution, and a locally Lipschitz\nmap from  to  gives a maximal nonlinear trajectory.\nEach compact time interval before its lifetime satisfies the weak wave\nequation and both initial traces. Pointwise power nonlinearities need their\nown mapping estimates, and the global subcritical and critical theories need\nmore than this construction.\n\nOn all of space, a globally Lipschitz reaction with  instead gives\na global forward solution for every  datum. The\nwhole-space Cauchy theorem\nidentifies both strong initial traces and the weak PDE, and proves uniqueness\nwithin the energy-continuous PDE class. Smooth compact Cauchy data on\n give a\nmaximal smooth wave\nfor every smooth scalar reaction fixing zero, and the nonlinear-wave section\nproves energy conservation for waves whose displacement slices are separately\ncompactly supported.\n\nFor symmetric first-order systems on all of space, the construction reaches\nthe original equation with  initial data and  forcing.\nThe coefficient entries lie in spatial , are continuous in\ntime in the  norm on the chosen interval, and have uniformly\nbounded spatial derivative norms. The solution has an \nbound and a continuous  initial trace. Under the same coefficient\nconditions, approximation also constructs a solution for rough \ninitial data and  forcing, with a continuous  representative.\nUniqueness holds among Bochner  classes satisfying the weak equation\nwith its initial boundary term. The construction satisfies both separated\ntests and smooth compactly supported spacetime tests.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.6. Coverage and the transition to fluids","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Coverage-and-the-transition-to-fluids"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations","header":"7.5. Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Energy-Uniqueness":{"contents":"\n\nUniqueness of weak waves (Evans §7.2.2) follows by testing the homogeneous\nequation against a backward time primitive and repeating the estimate on a\nfinite partition of the time interval. The argument here runs over an\nabstract Gelfand triple.\n\nFor  and a terminal time , set\n. Let a homogeneous weak wave have\nzero initial displacement and velocity at . Assume its principal form\n is symmetric, coercive with constant , and absolutely continuous\nin time, with coefficient derivative bound  and pivot-to-energy\nlower-order bound . Under the scalar integrability hypotheses\nfor the backward-energy identity, it satisfiesHere  denotes the continuous pivot-space displacement\nrepresentative. Zero initial data remove the initial boundary terms.\n\nThe weak equation is tested against a backward time primitive. Integration by\nparts exposes the endpoint pivot norm and the principal energy. Coercivity\ncontrols the primitive, while the coefficient derivative and lower-order form\nare estimated by their stated quadratic bounds.\n\nBy itself the estimate is not yet a Grönwall inequality, because\n ties the right-hand side to the endpoint\nquantity  with a coefficient of order . On an\ninterval short enough for that coefficient to be absorbed into the left-hand\nside, Grönwall's inequality gives  up to .\n\nLet  cover the time interval, with each\n short enough to absorb the right-hand side in\n. Then a homogeneous\nzero-data weak wave hasfor its continuous pivot-space displacement representative.\n\nThe backward estimate and Grönwall argument give vanishing on the first short\ninterval. The zero displacement and velocity traces at its right endpoint\nrestart the same argument on the next interval. Finite induction covers the\nwhole interval.\n\nUniqueness for general data follows from the homogeneous zero-data case by\nsubtraction, which is pure linear algebra on the weak-wave predicate.\n\nSuppose every homogeneous zero-data weak wave satisfies\n. If  and  have the same operator,\nforcing, displacement trace, and velocity trace, then their difference is such\na homogeneous solution, hence\n\nSubtract the two weak formulations. Linearity cancels the common forcing and\ninitial traces, so the difference is a homogeneous zero-data weak wave.\nApplying homogeneous uniqueness to its displacement, velocity, and\nacceleration gives equality of the corresponding components.\n\nOf the three scalar integrability conditions in the backward estimate, two\ncome from measurability and the coefficient bounds: the coefficient\nderivative and lower-order pairings along  and . For the\ndifference of two solutions, the\nhomogeneous weak equation identifies  with the negative\nacceleration pairing;  then gives integrability of the principal\npairing.\n\nIn the real Hilbert Gelfand-triple setting, write \nfor the inclusion and assume  on . Require  to be\nabsolutely continuous in operator norm, with  for almost\nevery . Both  and  must be almost everywhere\nstrongly measurable as operator-valued maps. For every ,\nassume  is symmetric and, for all ,where  and . Two weak waves for this operator\nwith equal forcing and initial traces then satisfyThe scalar integrability conditions inside the backward-energy identity are\nderived from the  trajectories, the coefficient bounds, and the weak\nequation.\n\nSubtract the two waves, construct a sufficiently fine uniform finite\npartition, apply the backward-energy estimate on each cell, restart from\nthe zero endpoint traces, and identify the vanishing continuous\nrepresentative with all three Bochner classes.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution","header":"7.3.2. Energy Uniqueness","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Energy-Uniqueness"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models--Weak-derivative-graph-spaces":{"contents":"The graph-space model stores a finite family of  classes indexed by\nsymmetric multi-indices, including the base coordinate. Compatibility is\nexpressed by the distributional integration-by-parts identity. The completed bundle stores\nthe base coordinate and every weak derivative through order .\n\nThis model is the one used for:\n\n* local and domain Sobolev spaces ;* weak formulations and energy estimates;* extension and trace;* difference quotients and Rellich compactness;* Nemytskii operations, multiplier rules, and coordinate changes;* zero-boundary closures such as  and .\n\nWeak-derivative identities are stated using ordered lists of coordinate\ndirections, because iterated distributional derivatives compose in a\nspecified order.  The completed graph-space bundle is instead indexed by\nsymmetric multi-indices: derivative words that differ only by a permutation\nshare one coordinate.  A chosen list representative connects each symmetric\nindex to the ordered weak-derivative calculus, and permutation invariance\nshows that the resulting weak derivative is independent of that choice.\n\nFor finite , the finite PiLp graph norm combines the  coordinate\nnorms in the expected  manner.  At , it takes their\nmaximum.  This is equivalent to the finite sum norm often written in analysis\ntexts, but every quantitative PDEAtlas estimate is stated for the actual\nPiLp norm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems\u0009The two Sobolev models","header":"4.6.1.1.1. Weak-derivative graph spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models--Weak-derivative-graph-spaces"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Abstract-Extension-Interfaces":{"contents":"The two basic extension interfaces live at the same two levels as the\nrepresentative and graph-space Sobolev models. Each contains only the data\nthat later theorems use.\n\nFor natural order , exponent , and domain\n, the representative-level extension property is\nthe existence of a mapsuch thatfor every representative-level Sobolev function .Only the existence of  and the almost-everywhere\nagreement of base functions are required; the map need not be linear or\nbounded, and its values outside  are unconstrained.The chosen global object is a global Sobolev representative. When \nis open, its weak derivatives restrict to , and almost-everywhere\nagreement of the base functions and uniqueness of weak derivatives identify\nthem with the derivatives of . This is the information used by the\nextension-and-restriction density theorems.\n\nThe graph-space interface asks for the same almost-everywhere agreement on\n, realized by a continuous linear map between the completed spaces,\nwhich is the form in which norm estimates and compactness travel from\n to .\n\nA graph-space extension operator of order  is a continuous linear mapwhose base coordinate agrees almost everywhere with the base coordinate of the\ninput on . The structure stores:* toContinuousLinearMap, the bounded linear map between the completed graph\n  spaces;* agree_ae, the almost-everywhere agreement statement for the base\n  coordinate with respect to Lebesgue measure restricted to .The proposition HasExtensionOperator is Nonempty applied to this structure:\nit asserts existence without selecting a canonical operator in theorem\nstatements.Agreement of the derivative coordinates is not stored separately. Both the\noutput and input graph bundles certify that their stored coordinates are weak\nderivatives of their base coordinates, so restriction of the global\nweak-derivative identities, followed by uniqueness on the open domain,\nrecovers derivative-coordinate agreement whenever it is required.\n\nNeither interface constrains the extension outside . Support of the\nextension in a prescribed neighborhood of  is recorded as\nseparate data because it comes from\na separate step, multiplication by a cutoff, which is available at every\norder.\n\nPrescribed support is data added to an extension operator. A supported\noperator over a set  consists of an extension\noperator together withfor the base coordinate of every input . This indicator identity is\nindependent of modifications on null sets and is equivalent to saying that the\nchosen base representative vanishes almost everywhere outside .At every Sobolev order , including  and , any bounded\nextension operator can be localized when\n is measurable,  is compact, and\n is an open neighborhood of . Choose a smooth compactly\nsupported cutoff  in  with  on\n, and defineBoundedness follows from boundedness of multiplication by  on\n, agreement is preserved because the cutoff equals one\non , and the cutoff support gives the indicator identity\n(Operator.withSupportIn).\n\nOne implication between the interfaces is needed later. Almost-everywhere\nagreement of base coordinates is all that survives the passage, and it is\nall the density argument uses.\n\nEvery bounded graph-space extension operator induces a representative-level\nextension.Given  in the representative model, first store its weak derivatives in\ngraph space by ofRepresentative, apply the bounded graph-space operator, and then forget\nthe global derivative coordinates with toRepresentative. The operator's\nbase-coordinate agreement and the model bridge show that the resulting global\nrepresentative equals  almost everywhere on .The converse would need linearity and norm control, which a\nrepresentative-level extension does not carry.\n\nSend the input representative to the domain graph space. Apply the\ncontinuous linear extension and convert the result back to a global\nrepresentative. Combine the extension operator's agree_ae field with the\nalmost-everywhere base-coordinate identity for ofRepresentative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Extension and Density on Regular Domains","header":"4.3.1.1. Abstract Extension Interfaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Abstract-Extension-Interfaces"}});